  "fixtureHTML": "<!doctype html><html lang=\"en\"><head><meta charset=\"utf-8\"><title>Worlds beyond our Sun · research notebook</title><meta name=\"description\" content=\"A locally authored demonstration inspired by NASA's public exoplanet and Webb research topics.\"><script type=\"application/ld+json\">{\"@context\":\"https://schema.org\",\"@type\":\"ScholarlyArticle\",\"name\":\"Worlds beyond our Sun\"}</script></head><body><main>\n<p>LOCAL AUTHORED DEMO · This is an original teaching fixture, not a live NASA page or a NASA announcement. Follow the source links to read current public research.</p>\n<h1>Worlds beyond our Sun</h1>\n<p>An exoplanet is a planet orbiting a star beyond our solar system. Researchers compare many kinds of evidence: a star's changing brightness, its slight wobble, and the light that passes through a planet's atmosphere.</p>\n<h2>Reading an atmosphere with Webb</h2>\n<p>When a planet crosses its star, a small fraction of starlight can filter through the planet's atmosphere. Webb's infrared instruments help scientists study patterns in that light. A spectral feature is a clue to interpret alongside uncertainty, clouds, chemistry, and repeated observations.</p>\n<p>Use this notebook to select a passage, invite a clearly labeled local simulated companion, and save a cited artifact. No live researcher or external AI service is connected.</p>\n<h2>Keep the evidence close</h2>\n<p>Ask what was measured, which model connects the measurement to a claim, and what another observation could rule out. This fixture reports no new discovery and supplies no invented dates, planet counts, or mission results.</p>\n<nav><a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/exoplanets/\">Explore NASA's exoplanet science</a><a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/webb/\">Read about the Webb mission</a></nav>\n</main></body></html>\n",
